// error
// #4563: what a `jsonDecode<T>` inside a GENERIC body still refuses, and that
// each refusal says WHICH thing is wrong instead of reading as a misspelled
// class name.
//
// The generic case is resolved through the instantiation ledger, so both
// refusals land at the CALL that instantiated the wrapper -- the line the reader
// can act on, since the `jsonDecode<T>` itself may sit in a module they do not
// own and cannot edit.
//
// Two causes, ONE DIAGNOSTIC EACH (#4592). A class that never opted in is the
// reader's to mark: E0145. A type argument nothing bound is already refused by
// SPEC 15.3's general rule (E0068, #4560), so the JSON pass hands that
// diagnostic its reason instead of raising a second one for the same mistake --
// its own E0145 twin still fires where E0068 cannot reach, on a method call or
// under an argument the checker could not type.
//
// The generic-CLASS shape is refused too, one file over in
// check_json_decode_generic_recv.bit: that refusal is raised before any Checker
// exists, and `bit check` reports the resolve phase alone when it fails, so the
// two families cannot be pinned in one file.
import { Json, jsonDecode } from "std/json"

class Plain {
  v: i64,
}

fn once<T>(j: Json): T! {
  return jsonDecode<T>(j)?
}

fn main() {
  let j = Json.JsonNull
  let a = once<Plain>(j) catch _ {
    return
  }
  let _ = once(j) catch _ {
    return
  }
  print("${a.v}\n")
}
